 and  support us and then they I was just  rereading the letter told us they  couldn't support a series that that  seemed to talk about a system and  celebrate unbridled market forces and I  thought give us a break what are they  talking about and it's sort of like the  character of the of the us that you  found in Europe and other places where  people think that somehow this is 19th  century Wild West capitalism when of  course what the United States is is a  pretty highly regulated welfare state  does a lot of its regulation through  litigation and indeed we could have a  discussion about how increasingly  regulated it becomes and certainly  within that if you look at companies and  compare them to where they were 20 or 30  years ago I think by many of the  measures in Adrienne's book you'll see  they're much much more regulated as  entities than they used to be a second  question that follows from that is this  world of sarbanes-oxley that we're in  which some would say is the biggest wave  of corporate governance regulation since  the Great Depression and there's a big  positive here you compare it to Japan  where they've not been able to deal with  these issues now for over a decade and  it shows the responsiveness and the  vitality of our system but I think I've  been surprised by the lack of attention  and helping to move this beyond it  really rather more quickly than might  have been expected but I find the  failure the relative lack of attention  to the law of unintended consequences  and all the things that have been set in  motion by it and you talk to people  involved in corporate governance and  there's a lot that's happened that that  that wasn't anticipated or expected I  think an issue that Adrian and his  co-author discussed is is of course core  or social responsibility and that's  clearly a very big question at  shareholders versus stakeholders and  it's kind of a constant pressure and I  think the book actually gives us context  for thinking about it I also think there  are a couple of key aspects of the  sovereignty question going back to your  period Adrian of the 1970s and ITT the  church hearings the whole message was  companies thou shalt not get involved in  the domestic affairs of sovereign states  well in a way that's now turned around  and companies are being told yes indeed  you've got to get involved in this  affairs of sovereign states on a whole  range of issues it's a very complex  question and it's one as given our  nature of litigation is going to alien  tort law and so forth it's going to  become a more complex issue and a very  difficult one for companies to deal with  and to find the right footing  I think another point that I would make  goes back to somebody who's quite  important I know inspiration for this  book ray Vernon and Raymond Vernon who  had been a professor at Harvard somebody  who was very influential on our work and  in his last book he pointed during the  sort of hype of the newly new economy  that where companies as much as critics  of companies were saying government's  didn't matter borders didn't matter with  long experience he had started his  career and enforcement in the SEC in the  1930s he was the one who chronicled and  created the study of multinational  corporations talked about the basic  clash between an economic system of a  global marketplace with companies and a  political system of sovereign states and  the inevitable clashes that will occur  and as we look at some of the dismal  results of investment of one kind or  another around the world we see the  truth of that and that that's going to  continue the last point I'd like to end  on adrian is with your friend Ishtar  from four thousand years ago and you  didn't pick up on that segue how can I  resist doing that there he was in Sumer  launching a venture capital fund  and of course the focus is on the Middle  East today in Iraq today and asking  what's going to happen is it going to be  a country on welfare for many years with  deep political social much violence or  will it evolve and it's very interesting  if any of you read the UN Development  Programme study on forgot the exact  title Arab Social Development in the  Middle East something like that which  really questioned about how the Middle  East has not been part of this process  of global integration of Trade and  connection partly you can say it's I  mean when they say its culture you could  say it's the resource curse which comes  from oil and so forth but there is a  question about how openness may lead in  surprising ways in Iraq in the region  and a point that I think that you would  make Adrian the importance of companies  their key point in creating civil  societies in breaking the monopoly of  the state and perhaps the picture might  end up looking different than some think  if we go back to that creating  circumstances that would allow Adam  Smith's observation about the propensity  to truck barter and exchange one thing  for another  and do it in a civil society with law in  Iraq and other countries perhaps we  would see more positive benefits than  some might expect so thank you very much  thank you Dan now like to open the floor  up for a few questions raise your hand  I'll recognize you  someone will come around with a mic wait  for the mic please then give your name  and a brief question address to one or  both of the speakers yeah this is a  question for Adrian in the early part of  your talk you talked about historic  opposition by libertarians to limited  liability corporations this is in that  vein do you see any meaningful  difference between a limited liability  corporation that comes about because of  contract Arian pronouncements to both  creditors and customers that says  remember that while you're dealing with  us we do not fact accept liability in a  broad sense versus limited liability  corporations which are legal devices as  they are today and then if you're if  there is a meaningful difference between  the two what's the viability of each  well we know what the viability of the  latter is of course but what's the  viability of the contract Arian legal  liability notion which would probably  more comfortable libertarians well a  little bit about the difference a little  bit more about what you think the  difference is one difference with me on  tort liability right that a purely  contract Arian firm could declare it to  its contractors that that liability for  deaths or for contracts would be limited  by third parties would fall outside of  that yeah that'd be one example but I  mean because libertarians you know  philosophically have always seen that if  limited liability corporations could  arise as a market phenomena through  contract area means there wouldn't be  those objections but because they're so  often a political device is where you  get that so is there any viability of a  limited liability corporation that comes  about contract Arian in its origin well  I think one has to deal with the world  that as it is and then and the simple  truth is that's not the path that we've  gone down that we  we went down a path essentially in which  the government's had an extraordinarily  intrusive role in corporate life up  until up until the mid 19th century that  it reserved the right of limited  liability to those organizations that  were doing the government's will they  were essentially given monopolies to run  railroads or to or to run canals  whatever and the road that was taken was  I think the easiest one that you could  have taken which was simply to move out  of that business of precisely regulating  companies and just just to create the  option of registering seven people  saying that they were going to be  company directors posting the fact that  your liability was limited posting an  address and doing all that sorta stuff  so I think I think it was it's a  compromise but it's a fairly it's a  fairly recognizable and easy compromise  to take now I recognize that all sorts  of libertarians have had problems with  that not least the fact that it means  that the obligations for debts tends to  be imposed on the rest of society rather  than on the people who are engaged in  the business contract but I think on  balance if you look at the way that  these organizations both I think created  a counterweight to the state and I think  this is it was a remarkable thing  there's up until the mid 19th century  essentially corporations have been  extensions of state power from the mid  19th century onwards they're rapidly  turned into constraints on state power  and secondly the way they spread around  the world with such speed and the way  that they do go from being marginal to  economic life to being absolutely  central to economic life in a matter of  30 or 40 years is an extraordinary  testimony to the viability of this of  this compromise that was produced  Phil thank you  Phil Harvey dkt Liberty project mr.  Wooldridge I'm curious as to why  companies began getting smaller in on or  about 1970 as I believe you said whether  you think that's a good thing and  whether you expect it to continue sure I  think that the logic of size is  determined by the whether it makes more  sense economic rata costs of doing  something in-house are bigger or smaller  than the transaction costs of going to  the market and I think really underlying  what's going on is that the transaction  costs of going to the market are  constantly getting lower because of  Technology because it's simply easier to  go to the market to get things done on  the other hand the bureaucratic costs of  doing things internally within a company  are not going down anything like the  like as quickly although they will go  down because of technology so there's an  overwhelming economic pressure for  companies to get smaller and I think  that will that will continue to go on I  think that the the top that the biggest  50 companies in the world have grown  much more slowly than the world economy  as a whole over the last 10 to 20 years  and I think also globalization is  crucial here because I think what  globalization does is exactly the  opposite of what the anti-globalization  people think it does what it does is  make it easier to raise capital make it  easier to buy and use technology and  make it easier to get your stuff to the  market because you no longer need to  have very close relationships with with  with local government officials all you  need to do is to be able to physically  get your stuff to the market and so it  makes that that shifts hugely the  balance of advantage away from  incumbents towards challengers and the  story that always intrigues me here is  the story of Nokia which up until the  early 1980s was basically a Finnish firm  stuck there in a slightly obscure part  of the world apologies to any Finn's  here for saying that and it was  producing wellington boots and paper and  pulp products and the then head  nokia visited Gorbachev and decided god  he had found his ideal market there was  Gorbachev in charge of all these people  who didn't have any lavatory paper here  he was running a paper and pulp company  he could provide them with lavatory  paper he became the laboratory paper  paper making giant of the world he  unfortunately committed suicide and he  was replaced by a man who saw the future  of that company in wireless telephones  and very very rapidly this company that  started out in Finland becomes a mobile  phone making giant so I think it's just  an illustration of the way that  challenges can get even if they're in in  peripheral parts of the world can get  into markets very very rapidly as long  as they have a good idea and and a good  corporate culture they can they can take  over or they can become major major  players in world markets and I think the  overall logic of that of this shift away  from incumbents towards challengers plus  the fact that transaction